    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Its author and date are debated: the oldest excavated fragments, the Guodian bamboo slips, date to around 300 BCE, and a relatively complete text probably existed by the mid third century BCE.</li><li>It describes the Tao as the root of all things and urges a return to harmony with it through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">wu wei</a>, simplicity and humility, in laconic, paradoxical verse.</li><li>Central to philosophical and religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, it was reread by Legalists, Confucians and Chinese Buddhists, and is one of the most translated texts in world literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The two silk copies found at Mawangdui in 1973 put the Te Ching before the Tao Ching, reversing the traditional order, so one English translation of them is titled Te-Tao Ching.</p></aside>
    </section>

