    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tao Te Ching, "Classic of the Way and its Virtue", is also known simply as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a> after its traditional author. Laozi, whose name means "Old Master", is a figure whose historical existence scholars debate. The first account of his life, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> by the Han historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, gives three stories: in one he is Li Er or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ruizong-of-tang">Li Dan</a>, an archivist and contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> who wrote a book in two parts at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxi">Yinxi</a>, keeper of a pass, before departing to the West.</p><p>Scholars give different dates. Alan Chan of the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy argues that archaeology indicates that bodies of sayings attributed to Laozi were committed to writing probably from the second half of the fifth century BCE, and that overlapping collections were consolidated during the fourth century BCE. No Western scholarship follows the traditional dating to the time of Confucius, and few now hold that none of the text predates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>.</p><p>The compound dao-de, "way and virtue", emerges only in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and terms such as "ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>" also point to a Warring States development or at least interpolation of parts of the text.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The text describes the Tao as the source and ideal of all existence: unseen but not transcendent, immensely powerful yet supremely humble, the root of all things. People, having desires and free will, often act unnaturally and upset its balance, and the work aims to lead readers back to a natural state in harmony with the Tao. It treats language and conventional wisdom as biased and artificial and uses paradox to sharpen the point.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">Wu wei</a>, literally "non-action", is a central idea, with meanings ranging from "not forcing" to "acting spontaneously". It explains ziran, harmony with the Tao, and sets simplicity and humility against selfish action; on a political level it means avoiding war, harsh laws and heavy taxes.</p><p>The modern text has 81 short chapters in two parts. The first, the Daojing, chapters 1–37, is often considered more metaphysical, and the second, the Dejing, chapters 38–81, more political. Unlike Confucian texts, its statements refer to no particular persons, times or places. The style is laconic, with few grammatical particles, and poetic, combining short declarative statements with deliberate contradictions that invite varied interpretations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most modern scholarship holds the text to be a compilation, as is typical of long early Chinese works. The Guodian slips of around 300 BCE contain only about 2,000 characters that correspond to it, divided only by section endings, and lack the last fifteen chapters, 67 to 81. Franklin Perkins argues that their absence is not coincidental and suggests that these chapters, with their support for punishment and lack of self-cultivation practices, were merged later.</p><p>Traditional sources report early versions of 64, 68 or 72 chapters. The Heshang Gong edition divided the text into its present 81 chapters and gave them titles, though this was not universally accepted until perhaps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Linguistic studies by William Baxter and Liu Xiaogan point to a date before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Chuci</a> and, for at least a substantial portion, before the Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>; Scott Cook's study of the Guodian texts supports this.</p><p>The title Tao Te Ching, which marked the work as a classic, was first applied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Emperor Jing of Han</a> (157–141 BCE), though it appears not to have been widely used until near the end of the Han. The earliest versions were written in seal script, later ones in clerical and regular script.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The late Warring States <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher">Xunzi</a> gives the first reference to Lao Dan as a philosopher under the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>, criticising him for having "insight into retreat and yielding, but no insight into firmness and trustworthiness." The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Feizi</a> contains the earliest known commentaries on the text. Its terms were reinterpreted by Legalist thinkers and Confucianists, and above all through Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, which arrived long after the text was in wide use.</p><p>The three main transmitted editions are named after early commentaries: those of Yan Zun, of the legendary Heshang Gong, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> philosopher Wang Bi (226–249). The Heshang Gong rose to prominence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> under imperial patronage in the fifth and sixth centuries. In 719 Liu Zhiji argued for Wang Bi's superiority, but the Tang state kept the Heshang Gong in place because Wang Bi was less widely used. In 731 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Emperor Xuanzong of Tang</a>, whose house claimed descent from Laozi, declared himself an authority on it.</p><p>Readings changed over time: the Heshang Gong understood it largely through longevity practices, the Tang through Buddhist logic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> through internal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> onward the text was transmitted mainly with the commentaries of Heshang Gong and Wang Bi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> scholars compared old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to preserve what they took to be the older text, and later scholars such as Yao Nai and Ma Xulun continued to argue that Wang Bi's was the standard.</p><p>Archaeology has since recovered manuscripts older than any received text. In the 1920s and 1930s Marc <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> and others found thousands of scrolls in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogao-caves">Mogao Caves</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, including more than 50 partial and complete manuscripts and a copy of the lost Xiang'er commentary. In 1973 the Mawangdui Silk Texts, from a tomb dated to 168 BCE, yielded two nearly complete copies, Text A and Text B, both placing the Te Ching first.</p><p>In 1993 the oldest known version was found on bamboo slips in a tomb near Guodian in Jingmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>: around 800 slips with over 13,000 characters. Both the Mawangdui and Guodian versions are generally consistent with the received texts apart from chapter order and graphic variants, and several recent translations draw on them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tao Te Ching is regarded, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>, as the foundational text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. It is central to both philosophical and religious Taoism and has been described as having greatly influenced Chinese culture, art and medicine more broadly.</p><p>First translated into French in the early nineteenth century, it is one of the most translated texts in world literature, next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>: over 250 times into Western languages by one count, and 1930 translations into 94 languages by another. The first English version, by the Scottish missionary John Chalmers in 1868, drew heavily on Stanislas Julien's French; James Legge later translated it for the Sacred Books of the East. Chinese scholars and teachers including Lin Yutang, D. C. Lau, Wing-tsit Chan and Gia-Fu Feng produced English versions in the twentieth century.</p><p>Classical Chinese, with no tense, number or person and little punctuation, makes it hard to translate, and many passages seem deliberately ambiguous. Popular interpretive versions are disputed: Russell Kirkland calls them Orientalist fantasies, while Michael LaFargue and Jonathan Herman argue that they meet a real spiritual need in the West.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Tao Te Ching, traditionally ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>, is a short Classical Chinese text compiled from sayings during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, known from bamboo slips of about 300 BCE. Teaching the Tao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">wu wei</a> and simplicity in paradoxical verse, it founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, shaped Chinese thought and has been translated more than almost any other book.</em></p>

